# Common blackbird

The common blackbird is the familiar thrush of European gardens and woodland,
the male all black with a yellow bill. The female builds a deep cup nest of dry
grass and small twigs, plasters the inside with mud and lines it with finer
grass, usually in a hedge or a creeper against a wall. Two or three broods are
raised in a season, and the nest is built afresh for each. The rich, fluting
song is delivered from a high perch from late winter onward. See [[turdidae]].
